---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'install', label: 'Install' },
   { id: 'screen-reader', label: 'Screen reader output' },
   { id: 'axe', label: 'axe runs' },
   { id: 'coverage', label: 'Coverage' },
   { id: 'agents', label: 'Agent tools' },
   { id: 'interfaces', label: 'Interfaces' },
   { id: 'next', label: 'Start here' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Test what the screen reader says"
   summary="a11ied drives VoiceOver, NVDA, and a virtual screen reader from the command line, runs axe against a URL, and reports which WCAG criteria automation can decide on its own. One install gives you a CLI, an MCP server, and a TypeScript API over the same runtime."
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="split panel" id="install">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Install</p>
            <h2>Two commands to a working setup</h2>
         </div>
         <pre><code>npm install --global a11ied

a1 doctor</code></pre>
         <p>
            <code>doctor</code> checks this machine and leads with the verdict: what still needs
            to run, then each screen reader target with its checks, then the browser a11ied will
            automate.
         </p>
         <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>✗ 1 setup step needed before a1 is fully usable on macOS 26.0 (arm64)

Action items
  1. Install the Guidepup VoiceOver preferences bundle
     cd "/usr/local/lib/node_modules/a11ied" && npx -y @guidepup/setup install

Targets
  ✗ macos-voiceover  requires setup  1 setup step is missing.
      ✓ AppleScript control enabled in VoiceOver settings
      ✓ AppleScript control confirmed by macOS
      ✓ VoiceOver local preferences exist
      ✓ VoiceOver welcome dialog suppressed
      ✗ Guidepup VoiceOver preferences bundle installed
      ✓ Screen recording available to the current host app
  - windows-nvda     Only available on Windows.
  ✓ virtual-dom      ready  No setup needed.</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">No setup needed</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            The virtual target is ready as soon as the package installs. VoiceOver and NVDA
            need <code>a1 setup</code> once per machine; it runs the Guidepup commands
            <code>doctor</code> lists and re-checks when they finish.
         </p>
         <p class="aside-note">
            Drop <code>--global</code> to install into a project and use the TypeScript API.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="screen-reader">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Screen reader output</p>
         <h2>Read the announcement order for a page</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         Start a session, step through the page, and read the phrase log. Every step returns the
         spoken text as JSON.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr start --target virtual --allow-virtual --url http://localhost:3000/checkout --json
a1 sr next --session drv_c33f1d8f --json
a1 sr read --session drv_c33f1d8f --json</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>"spokenPhraseLog": [
  "document",
  "heading, Checkout, level 1",
  "form",
  "Email",
  "textbox, Email, required",
  "Card number",
  "textbox, Card number",
  "button, Pay now",
  "button",
  "end of form"
]</code></pre>
      <p>
         The log is the order a screen reader user moves through. This one shows the required state
         on the email field, and a ninth entry that announces as <code>button</code> with no name.
      </p>
      <p>
         Swap <code>--target virtual --allow-virtual</code> for <code>--target voiceover</code> to
         run the same steps against the real screen reader.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="axe">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">axe runs</p>
         <h2>Run axe against a URL</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         <code>--level AA</code> resolves the 72 axe rules mapped to WCAG 2.2 level AA and runs
         them against the page.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 axe --url http://localhost:3000/checkout --level AA --json</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>"violations": [
  &#123;
    "id": "button-name",
    "impact": "critical",
    "help": "Buttons must have discernible text",
    "helpUrl": "https://dequeuniversity.com/rules/axe/4.13/button-name",
    "nodes": [
      &#123;
        "target": ["button[type=\"button\"]"],
        "html": "&lt;button type=\"button\"&gt;&lt;img src=\"cart.png\" alt=\"\"&gt;&lt;/button&gt;",
        "failureSummary": "Fix any of the following:\n  Element does not have inner text that is visible to screen readers\n  aria-label attribute does not exist or is empty"
      &#125;
    ]
  &#125;
]</code></pre>
      <p>
         Scope a run three other ways: <code>--criterion 1.4.3</code> for one success criterion,
         <code>--rule color-contrast --rule link-name</code> for named rules, or no selector at all
         for every rule mapped in the WCAG version.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel" id="coverage">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Coverage</p>
            <h2>Find out what axe cannot decide</h2>
         </div>
         <p>
            Ask what a criterion needs before you test it. a11ied ships the W3C ACT mapping and the
            axe rule tags as local data, so the lookup runs without a network request.
         </p>
         <pre><code>a1 wcag 2.4.3 --json</code></pre>
         <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>"coverage": &#123;
  "coverageState": "hybrid",
  "axeRuleIds": [],
  "actRuleIds": [],
  "notes": ["Focus order depends on interaction flow, not just static structure."]
&#125;,
"strategy": &#123;
  "preferredEvidenceMode": "hybrid",
  "procedureIds": ["focus_order_probe"],
  "requiresRealTarget": true
&#125;</code></pre>
         <p>
            No axe rule decides Focus Order. The strategy names the probe to run and says the test
            needs a real target, which is what the driver session provides.
            <a href="/coverage">Coverage</a> explains the four states and the mapping behind them.
         </p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">WCAG 2.2, level AA</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            24 success criteria. axe rules decide 8. Four are hybrid. Twelve stay manual.
         </p>
         <p class="aside-note">
            Across all three levels: 86 criteria, 28 automated, 10 hybrid, 48 manual.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="agents">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Agent tools</p>
         <h2>Give a coding agent the same commands</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         The MCP server runs over stdio and opens no network port. Add it to any MCP client and the
         agent gets criterion lookup, applicability inspection, axe runs, and driver sessions.
      </p>
      <pre data-label="CONFIG"><code>{`{
  "mcpServers": {
    "a11ied": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "a11ied", "mcp"]
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="AI AGENT PROMPT"><code>Test http://localhost:3000/checkout with a11ied.
Run axe at level AA, then step through the form with the screen reader.
Report the axe violations and the spoken phrase log separately.</code></pre>
      <p>
         The <a href="/guides/agent-skill">agent skill</a> adds the reasoning: check coverage first,
         pick the right target, and keep automated results apart from transcripts.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="ledger" id="interfaces">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Interfaces</p>
         <h2>One runtime, three ways in</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong><a href="/reference/cli">CLI</a></strong>
            <span>The <code>a1</code> command. Every subcommand takes <code>--json</code> and prints one structured object.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong><a href="/reference/mcp">MCP server</a></strong>
            <span>Eight tools and seven read-only resources over stdio, for coding agents and editors.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong><a href="/reference/api">TypeScript API</a></strong>
            <span>Import <code>runAxe</code>, <code>startDriverSession</code>, and the WCAG lookups from <code>a11ied</code>.</span>
         </div>
      </div>
      <p>
         The MCP tools and the CLI commands call the same functions. A result from one matches a
         result from any other.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="next">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Start here</p>
         <h2>Run your first test</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         The <a href="/quickstart">quickstart</a> takes a local URL from an axe run to a screen
         reader transcript in about five minutes. Read <a href="/targets">targets</a> first if you
         want to know when to use VoiceOver instead of the virtual reader.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
